%% See quipmachinery.dg for how this should work
%% A quip definition should look like:
%% #q-whatever
%% (available *) When it should be available
%% (* has priority NUMBER) If it should be weighted more heavily (default 1)
%% (* line 1) The first line of the quip
%% (* line 2) The second line of the quip
%% You can also use (* line) if it has only a single line
%% (* involves TOPIC) If it involves any topics, used for discourse structure

%% There are no quips in acts 0 or 4, so this file is divided into sections for 1, 2, and 3

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%% ACT 1
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

#q-1-comms
(quip *)
(* involves #t-comms)
(* line 1)
	(div @chameleon) { So, not to ask a stupid question, but I (span @italic) am curious. Why are we doing all of this via text? }
	(div @spider) { Bandwidth. The less data needs to be transmitted, the better it can be hidden and secured. This system uses approximately one bit per letter, or one byte per word. Voice would be an order of magnitude more. }
	(div @jackdaw) { plus he sent that in like two seconds so either he types faster than liszt or he had it written up and ready to go }
	(div @spider) { It all comes down to information theory. And in this case a large corpus of my writing and a chorded keyboard. }
	(div @jackdaw) { i regret asking }
	(div @chameleon) { I don’t. }
(* line 2)
	(div @spider) { Besides, the one bit per letter is only an average. Some people’s messages can be compressed even further. It all comes down to how predictable they are—for example, Jackdaw is about to say “---- (space) you”. }
	(div @jackdaw) { wow (space) ---- (space) you }

